<p>The “Red Trail” designation was used from 1913 until North Dakota replaced road colors with numbers in 1917, although color designations continued appearing on maps after 1921. Trail names have recently resurfaced for their historical associations and nostalgic appeal, not necessarily for geographic accuracy. In the Mandan region, “Red Trail” now names an elementary school, a Stark County ethanol plant, a northeastern Mandan street, and a promoted scenic route.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/red-trail-state-route-3/300-1921-nd-hwy-map-colors-derated.jpg" alt="1921 North Dakota highway map showing color-designated routes">
    <figcaption>North Dakota highway map showing color-designated routes, 1921</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>When colors identified state highways, the Red Trail began at Pembina and followed the Red River Valley past Fargo to Fairmount before turning west toward Oakes. Early maps sometimes called the road along North Dakota's eastern border the Meridian Highway because it followed the Sixth Principal Meridian. Texas businesses promoted that route as part of a north–south Pan-American Highway. Red Trail also designated the east–west road across southern North Dakota, from Fargo through Jamestown, Mandan–Bismarck, and Dickinson to the Montana border near Beach.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/red-trail-state-route-3/366-red-trail.jpg" alt="Map of the Red Trail through the Mandan area">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>The Red Trail entered Mandan from the Missouri River, either from the original landing south of the automobile-bridge approach or the later access in today's Captain's Landing Township. It meandered west and north across open land south of the railroad tracks, followed the tracks, and crossed them at grade near present-day BisMan Avenue, east of North Star Steel on the Strip. The trail then continued west to Main Street.</p>

<p>At Sixth Avenue Northwest, originally McCullough Avenue, the route turned north toward higher, drier ground above the Heart River. At the northern bluffs near today's Sunset Drive and Old Red Trail, it turned northwest before eventually leaving Mandan directly westward. Early roads generally followed established trails because they offered gentle grades and easy stream crossings. Improved equipment and materials later made more direct routes possible, and many earlier roads were abandoned. Wheel ruts remain visible along some historic routes, including the Bismarck–Deadwood stagecoach trail.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/red-trail-state-route-3/708-overview-map-jun24.jpg" alt="Map comparing three east–west routes through the Mandan and Bismarck area">
    <figcaption>Comparison of three heavily traveled east–west routes through the Mandan–Bismarck area</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<h3>Early recorded routes</h3>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/red-trail-state-route-3/370-1911-postal-route-map-morton-county.jpg" alt="1911 postal-route map of Morton County">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>As frontier settlement expanded beyond rivers, streams, and railroad stations, overland roads supplemented water travel. Military expeditions documented their routes on maps. Trails were often named for the people or agencies that blazed them or the events that established them. The U.S. Post Office adapted many Army maps for early postal routes. These overland “trails” were often little more than worn paths through grass and were barely adequate for horses, stagecoaches, and animal-drawn wagons.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/red-trail-state-route-3/388-2008-morton-couty-historic-trails-map-cropped.jpg" alt="Map of historic trails in Morton County">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>{% place "fort-abraham-lincoln" %}Fort McKeen, later Fort Abraham Lincoln{% endplace %}, was established to support the railroad's westward expansion. The 1873 national financial crisis forced the railroad to pause construction at Bismarck, east of the Missouri. The fort's mission shifted toward scouting a Black Hills location for a new post to support the continuing “Indian War”; that work identified the future Fort Meade site. These assignments again required overland travel through previously unmapped territory.</p>

<h3>Roads designated by colors and numbers</h3>

<p>Before 1913, city and county supervisors maintained local roads. When North Dakota created its State Highway Commission and Highway Department, colors became the first route designations for roads that were often scarcely more than two tracks. The state began coordinated highway planning and authorized preparation of its first highway map.</p>

<p>In 1917, the Highway Department replaced colors with numbers. The route from Fargo through Mandan to Dickinson, Medora, and Beach became State Route 3. In 1921, the department adopted even numbers for east–west highways and odd numbers for north–south routes, renaming State Route 3 as Highway 6.</p>

<h3>Ice crossings, boats, and ferries</h3>

<p>The Missouri River was the route's greatest obstacle. During the winters of 1878–79 and 1879–80, Northern Pacific bridge engineer {% person "thomas-rosser" %}Thomas Rosser{% endperson %} laid temporary tracks across the frozen river. Construction supplies, locomotives, and other rolling stock crossed the ice while the railroad extended its line west and built its permanent bridge.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/red-trail-state-route-3/300-1879-train-on-river.jpg" alt="Train crossing the frozen Missouri River in 1879">
  </figure>
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/red-trail-state-route-3/325-1879-crossing-missouri-on-ice.jpg" alt="Missouri River ice crossing in 1879">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>During warmer months, boats carried passengers and freight until permanent bridges were available. Ferries remained the principal local crossing for many travelers until an automobile bridge opened in 1922, and landing sites moved as equipment and needs changed.</p>

<p>John McGinley was among Mandan's earliest ferry operators, rowing passengers and their belongings in a small skiff during the 1870s and early 1880s. Bismarck's earliest landing stood west of Front Avenue, then called Front Street. The 1909 road from the State Capitol to Fort Abraham Lincoln, constructed by state-penitentiary labor, incorporated this crossing. A later east-bank landing north of the warehouse district and above the steamboat dry dock offered rail access and a better road.</p>

<p>The west bank also had several landings. Heart River access supported the Motsiff operation south of Mandan, near today's Sitting Bull Bridge, Dogtown, and Stage Stop Bar &amp; Grill, serving both Mandan and Fort Lincoln. In 1878, a spur reached the west bank opposite Bismarck's steamboat landing so rail sidings could operate on both sides. After the railroad bridge opened in 1882, trains carried most commercial freight and some passengers, though many residents walked across the bridge between trains instead of paying for the ferry.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/red-trail-state-route-3/446-1909-car-ferry.jpg" alt="Automobile ferry between Mandan and Bismarck in 1909">
    <figcaption>Mandan–Bismarck automobile ferry, 1909</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>In 1916, the Mandan Automobile Club and Bismarck Commercial Club purchased Walter Gossard's gasoline-powered ferry. The boosters paid $2,000 down and raised the remaining $1,300. They formed a corporation capitalized at $2,000 and expected daily receipts above $25 to repay subscribers by summer's end.</p>

<p>Twelve trains traveled in each direction every day in 1916, with special trains added for events such as opera performances and Mandan's State Fair. Ferry revenue fell as trains carried more wagons and automobiles. The City of Mandan classified the ferry as an essential service and subsidized it. The last ferry, <em>Marion</em>, ended service in 1922.</p>

<h3>Auto trails and the Good Roads movement</h3>

<p>Through the 1920s, local booster organizations urged governments to improve roads that were often merely scraped earth. Auto trails frequently followed historic routes chosen for level terrain and shallow crossings. North Dakota examples included Custer's Trail, Old Trails Highway, and the Bismarck–Deadwood stagecoach trail. Hundreds of associations promoted an overlapping, sometimes disconnected patchwork. Some routes favored towns that paid association dues rather than efficient travel. Signs and route names were not standardized, and road maps were scarce.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/red-trail-state-route-3/125-aaa-logo-600dpi.jpg" alt="American Automobile Association emblem">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Nine automobile clubs met in Chicago in 1902 to coordinate advocacy for better roads. The American Automobile Association gathered and distributed road information and formally incorporated as an umbrella organization in 1910. It promoted a nationwide improved-road network, roadside services no more than 120 miles apart, and maps for motorists.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/red-trail-state-route-3/150-anton-westguard-closeup.jpg" alt="Portrait of highway pathfinder Anton L. Westgard">
    <figcaption>Anton L. Westgard</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Norwegian immigrant Anton L. Westgard, known as the Great Pathfinder, crossed the continent three times in 147 days in a stock automobile while collecting data for AAA maps. His 1911 field survey established a transcontinental route from New York along the Hudson River and Great Lakes and through Omaha, Denver, Salt Lake City, and Sacramento. The route became known as the Lincoln Highway.</p>

<p>Westgard joined the National Highways Association in 1913. As its vice president, he left New York on a 17,000-mile survey intended to provide evidence for federal road construction. His work included studying a route for the future National Parks Highway. Early proposals favored a northern North Dakota route through Grand Forks, Minot, and Williston, avoiding the need for an automobile bridge at Bismarck and offering direct access to Glacier National Park. A southern route ultimately prevailed because it included the state's largest city, capital, and national memorial park near Medora.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/red-trail-state-route-3/334-1922-national-park-to-park-hwy-map.jpg" alt="1922 map of highways connecting western national parks">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>The National Parks Highway Association, founded in 1913, promoted an improved northern route to Yellowstone National Park and established state chapters, including a North Dakota office on Bismarck's Main Street. National Park Service director Stephen Mather envisioned a circuit linking 13 western parks. The National Park-to-Park Highway Association formed in 1915 to advance that plan, aided in part by Westgard. A 1922 map labeled North Dakota's former Red Trail—then Highway 6—as the National Parks Highway.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/red-trail-state-route-3/424-1911-old-trails-road-sketch-map-dav.jpg" alt="1911 sketch map of the Old Trails Road">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Although AAA identified several cross-country auto trails, early national road-building efforts emphasized transcontinental routes to California for San Francisco's 1915 Panama-Pacific International Exposition and San Diego's Panama-California Exposition, held through 1917. Large rural areas remained underserved.</p>

<h3>The automobile bridge and Memorial Highway</h3>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/red-trail-state-route-3/708-1938-17aug-nd-memorial-bridge-overhead.jpg" alt="Aerial view of the North Dakota Memorial Bridge and its approaches">
    <figcaption>Aerial view of the paved bridge approaches and surrounding gravel roads, August 17, 1938</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>The {% place "north-dakota-memorial-bridge" %}North Dakota Memorial Bridge{% endplace %}, later called the Liberty Memorial Bridge, opened in 1922 as the first automobile bridge across the Missouri in the upper Midwest. It carried State Route 3 and honored the men and women who served and died in World War I. When North Dakota adopted federal highway numbers in 1926, the route became part of U.S. Highway 10.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/red-trail-state-route-3/433-1922-finney-drug-postcard-screenshot.jpg" alt="Postcard view of the North Dakota Memorial Bridge in 1922">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Traffic east of the bridge initially followed its curved brick approach to Front Avenue. The four miles between western Bismarck and eastern Mandan were largely rural. In 1924, the road between the cities was paved as a two-lane concrete highway.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/red-trail-state-route-3/210-m-hwy-marker-derated.jpg" alt="Memorial Highway marker">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>On May 30, 1925, the bridge and four-mile road between Front Avenue at Hannifin Street in Bismarck and Main Street in Mandan were renamed Memorial Highway to honor those who died in World War I. The Women's Auxiliary of Bismarck's American Legion post donated a copper plaque mounted on a granite fieldstone monument at Front Avenue and Hannifin Street, where it remains.</p>

<p>A three-block dogleg beneath a railroad viaduct connected Bismarck's West Main Avenue, north of the tracks, with Front Avenue to the south. A new viaduct over West Main Avenue opened in 1959, creating a direct paved connection. Improved access encouraged 1960s businesses west of the river to advertise “Zip to the Strip” and “On the Strip,” giving the commercial district its nickname. This part of rural Morton County was annexed by Mandan in 1974. The older dogleg viaduct was removed during the 1994 Washington Street–Main Avenue reconstruction.</p>

<h3>U.S. Highway 10 and the old routes</h3>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/red-trail-state-route-3/334-1937-nd-highway-map-from-wpa-project.jpeg" alt="1937 North Dakota highway map">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>In 1926, North Dakota Highway 6 became part of U.S. Highway 10, the northernmost coast-to-coast federal highway. The standardized national route was eligible for federal construction, maintenance, and improvement funds. As traffic grew, sections were relocated to better alignments and federal support ended for abandoned portions.</p>

<p>After completion of the Interstate Highway System created duplicate routes, the federal government withdrew support from many older U.S. highways. In 1986, U.S. Highway 10's designation in North Dakota was removed west of West Fargo.</p>

<p>In Morton County, Main Street west from Sixth Avenue Northwest is commonly called Old Highway 10 and officially designated County Road 139. From the same intersection, the former Red Trail turned north on Sixth Avenue, overlapping the Mandan street named Old Red Trail north of Interstate 94. Beyond the city it becomes County Road 139A. Roads 139 and 139A rejoin near New Salem, and County Road 139 continues to the county line. Stark County designates its Old Highway 10 route as County Road 10.</p>
